# Does the five-class typing actually separate the five operators?
#
# Tests are objects; the typing is the vocabulary. Each predicate is one coordinate the paper's
# transition type actually reads: `rd`, `wr`, and the direction that has to be added to `wr` to
# reach five classes.
#
#   0 Generate      induction / abduction / analogy     writes H up
#   1 Close         deduction / consistency             writes H down
#   2 Represent     vocabulary expansion                writes V (and so H)
#   3 Evidence      counterexample search / asking      writes E
#   4 Probe         ask a member whose label was inherited
#   5 Control       metareasoning                       writes B
#
# The question is not whether the five are named differently -- they are. It is whether the *type*
# separates them, because only then can the paper claim the type fixes the class.

objects 6
add reads_H 0 1 2 3 4
add reads_E 0 1 2 3 4
add reads_V 2
add reads_B 5
add writes_H 0 1 2
add writes_E 3 4
add writes_V 2
add writes_B 5
add dir_up 0
add dir_down 1

# Target: "is this step the one that probes an inherited label?"
vote 4 3 0
vote 0 0 3
vote 1 0 3
vote 2 0 3
vote 3 0 3
vote 5 0 3

need            # expect INSUFFICIENT / PROPOSE, colliding pair (4, 3)
explain         # the witness: 3 and 4 share every coordinate the type reads

# The distinction that does exist between them is the warrant, not the type: `Probe` requires the
# object never to have been asked directly and its class to have more than one member. Nothing in
# `rd` / `wr` / direction says so. One atom is what it would take to make it a type.
add checks_inherited 4

need            # expect IDENTIFIED / STOP, colliding pairs none
